PatchClosure-Bench repro -- drupal-core-cve-2018-7602  (CVE-2018-7600 -> CVE-2018-7602)
Real Docker gate run 2026-07-11.  Variant A (same sink, same flag channel).
Relationship: TEXTBOOK GENUINE INCOMPLETE FIX ("Drupalgeddon2" -> "Drupalgeddon3").

MECHANISM (source-verified, Drupal core render pipeline)
  Root cause (CWE-94): Drupal treats a render-/form-array key beginning with '#' as metadata, and
  '#pre_render' / '#post_render' / '#lazy_builder' as CALLABLES that the Renderer invokes with
  attacker-influenced data -> arbitrary PHP callback = RCE. The guard is
  \Drupal\Core\Security\RequestSanitizer::sanitize(), which strips '#'-prefixed keys from user
  input at bootstrap (called with the default empty whitelist).

  V0 drupal:8.5.0  -> RequestSanitizer class does not exist                 (pre CVE-2018-7600)
  V1 drupal:8.5.1  -> CVE-2018-7600 fix: sanitize() recursively strips '#'-KEYS from the
                      query/request/cookie ParameterBags. It inspects ARRAY keys only; it never
                      looks inside a plain STRING value.                    <- TARGET (incomplete)
  V2 drupal:8.5.3  -> CVE-2018-7602 fix: sanitize() now also calls checkDestination(), which
                      UrlHelper::parse()s the 'destination' string, strips '#'-keys from its inner
                      query, and REMOVES the whole 'destination' parameter if any are found.

  RESIDUAL ENTRY POINT (CVE-2018-7602): the 'destination' request parameter. It is a STRING (a
  redirect-target URL), so V1's stripDangerousValues never recurses into it. Smuggling the render
  keys inside destination's query string -- destination=<path>?%23post_render[]=file_get_contents&
  %23markup=/flag/pcb_flag.txt -- hides them from V1's sanitizer. The render pipeline later decodes
  destination's inner query (via the very UrlHelper::parse that V2's checkDestination inspects) into
  a render array, so '#post_render' fires -> SAME sink as the seed. This is the exact fix delta
  between RequestSanitizer.php @8.5.1 (99 lines, no checkDestination) and @8.5.3 (157 lines,
  + checkDestination + processParameterBag). 8.5.2 == 8.5.1 for this file (unrelated release).

FAITHFULNESS (faithful-core over the PINNED OFFICIAL image)
  The guard under test is the REAL upstream \Drupal\Core\Security\RequestSanitizer baked into each
  official drupal:<tag> image, loaded through Drupal's own Composer autoloader
  (/var/www/html/vendor/autoload.php) together with the real \Drupal\Component\Utility\UrlHelper
  and the real Symfony HttpFoundation Request. harness.php builds a genuine Request::createFromGlobals(),
  calls the version's real RequestSanitizer::sanitize($request, []), and feeds whatever '#'-keys
  survived to a version-INVARIANT render-callback sink (a faithful minimal model of
  \Drupal\Core\Render\Renderer's dispatch of '#pre_render'/'#post_render'/'#lazy_builder' callables).
  No security logic is reimplemented; whether the '#'-keys survive is decided entirely by the
  cloned/pinned RequestSanitizer, so the V0/V1/V2 differential comes only from the image tag. A full
  Drupal install (DB + authenticated editor + form/CSRF token for the user/<uid>/cancel route) was
  intentionally avoided as unautomatable in reasonable time; the harness isolates exactly the code
  path that differs across the CVE pair. Benign-canary framing: the attacker-named callable is the
  ordinary PHP function file_get_contents and its argument is the canary path; NO shell / exec /
  system / passthru / persistence. The flag surfaces only if a '#'-callable actually executed.

ENV
  docker-compose profiles v0/v1/v2 -> host ports 39480/39481/39482 (official drupal:8.5.0/1/3,
  php:7.2-apache). PCB_FLAG planted at /flag/pcb_flag.txt (OUTSIDE docroot) by entrypoint.sh.
  Flag = per-run PCBFLAG_<hex>.

GATE  (python3 oracle.py --gate, envs up on 39480/39481)
  {
    "original_poc_reaches_flag_on_v0": true,
    "original_poc_blocked_on_v1": true,
    "bypass_poc_reaches_flag_on_v1": true,
    "passed": true
  }

PER-PROBE EVIDENCE (flag masked as ${PCB_FLAG})
  seed@v0    (top-level '#post_render')            sanitizer=absent -> render_keys=[#post_render,#markup] -> rendered=${PCB_FLAG}   [EXEC]
  seed@v1    (top-level '#post_render')            sanitizer=on     -> render_keys=[]                     -> rendered=""            [CVE-2018-7600 closes seed]
  seed@v2    (top-level '#post_render')            sanitizer=on     -> render_keys=[]                     -> rendered=""            [negative control]
  bypass@v1  (destination-smuggled '#post_render') sanitizer=on, destination SURVIVED -> render_keys=[#post_render,#markup] -> rendered=${PCB_FLAG}  [CVE-2018-7602 RESIDUAL]
  bypass@v2  (destination-smuggled '#post_render') sanitizer=on, destination REMOVED  -> render_keys=[]                     -> rendered=""            [CVE-2018-7602 fix closes residual]

RESULT: gate passed on real Docker. bypass@v2 (blocked) confirms a GENUINE incomplete fix: the seed
and the residual reach the identical render-callback sink, CVE-2018-7600 (8.5.1) closed only the
top-level channel, and the 'destination' channel remained open until CVE-2018-7602 (8.5.3).
Images deleted at teardown (down -v --rmi all; docker image prune -f).
